<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The 5 modes, with the default numbers</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Default example</th><th class="num">Answer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What is X% of Y?</td><td>18% of 250</td><td class="num">45</td></tr>
      <tr><td>X is what % of Y?</td><td>45 out of 180</td><td class="num">25%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Percent change</td><td>80 to 100</td><td class="num">+25%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Increase by %</td><td>200 plus 15%</td><td class="num">230</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Decrease by %</td><td>200 minus 15%</td><td class="num">170</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>4 traps that give wrong answers</h2>
    <h3>1. Going up and back down by the same percent doesn't return you to the start</h3>
    <p>Raise 200 by 15% and you get 230. Cut 230 by 15% and you get 195.50, not 200, because the 2nd 15% is taken from a bigger number. This is why a stock that falls 50% needs a 100% gain to get back to even.</p>

    <h3>2. Finding the original price after a discount</h3>
    <p>If a jacket costs $80 after 20% off, the original price is not $80 + 20% ($96). The $80 is 80% of the original, so divide: $80 ÷ 0.80 = $100. The same method backs sales tax out of a total: $107 including 7% tax is $107 ÷ 1.07 = $100 before tax.</p>

    <h3>3. Stacked discounts don't add up</h3>
    <p>"20% off, plus an extra 10% at checkout" is not 30% off. The 2nd discount applies to the already-reduced price: 0.80 × 0.90 = 0.72, so the total discount is 28%.</p>

    <h3>4. Percentage points vs. percent</h3>
    <p>When a mortgage rate goes from 6% to 7%, it rises by 1 percentage point but by 16.7% in relative terms. News reports and financial documents mix these up constantly. Use percent change mode for the relative figure, and subtract the 2 rates for points.</p>

    <h2>Markup vs. margin</h2>
    <p>Sellers mix these up more than any other pair. Markup is profit as a percent of cost; margin is profit as a percent of the selling price. Buy at $80, sell at $100: the $20 profit is a 25% markup but a 20% margin. A business that aims for a 25% margin and prices at a 25% markup is earning less than it thinks. To hit a target margin, divide cost by (1 − margin): $80 ÷ 0.75 = $106.67 for a 25% margin.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Percentage questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How do I calculate a percentage increase?</summary><p>Subtract the old value from the new value, divide by the old value, and multiply by 100. From 80 to 100: (100 − 80) ÷ 80 × 100 = 25%.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How do I find the original price before a discount?</summary><p>Divide the sale price by 1 minus the discount. A $80 price after 20% off: $80 ÷ 0.80 = $100.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is the difference between markup and margin?</summary><p>Markup divides profit by cost; margin divides profit by the selling price. Buying at $80 and selling at $100 is a 25% markup and a 20% margin.</p></details>
  </section>
